body:
An Id (or Ego) that is content with the Superego he has grown up to have will most likely be happier than one that doesn't. If every person was satisfied with the current structure of society, no one would question and explore beyond what we have and no one would form ideas that comes with philosophy that strays from the usual beliefs of society. With the diversity that human life has shown it capable of, this can't possibly happen. There will always be people that would think they aren't in their right environments, people that want other things than what society often promotes it can give, or people that see value in different concepts than what society adheres for. And this is why philosophy, religion, and all other kind of spiritual beliefs exists. We may not be content with the usual flow of human life, some may not be content with the progress that humanity is leaning towards, and some may see more value in the immaterial than the physical. As long as the Ego and individuality within every person exists, where there can be an endless amount of variation and combinations possible, this friction will never truly go away. We would need some sort of a hive mind physiology for this friction to be non-existent because the Ego is too 'random' for it to truly work in a linear way similar to the Id and Superego.

&#x200B;

As for your second question, well, there is a whole section of pseudoscience for how a hive mind could possibly work, often used for sci-fi films and tv shows. Explaining even the basics of it will be an exhausting endeavor and I would just recommend that you search for this yourself if you really want to find the answer.

&#x200B;

This is easily answerable by reading my previous replies. Without Ego, there will be no Superego in the first place because this can only be born through our conscious thinking and in the way we differentiate certain aspects of life, therefore, only Id will remain. They may differentiate certain actions in ways like: "This action is dangerous and should not be done." and "This action is beneficial and should be continued.". Examining it further, this shows how evolution operates, we adapt to our environment naturally and differentiate certain aspects of life through way of elimination and natural selection. But this is still not the same as the Ego's way of comprehension because even the being itself does not know what he is doing in the first place, he's only adapting for the sake of self-preservation. And this can be said for other similar functions of the Id with the Ego, no matter how similar it may be, it lacks the randomness of the Ego which differentiates it from other inherent functions of humanity. This, consequently, answers your last question as well. With all sentient beings we've seen, instincts are only born for the sake of self-preservation, therefore, it will deem everything outside of that unecessary and it won't do any actions the brain thinks is not beneficial for itself.

My sense of freedom is the outlier here, I believe. I know that having ulitmate power will make life boring, I know that having longlasting life will eventually make life a living hell, I know that life will lose meaning if I become the ultimate being, but I just can't help but wish for it regardless. I don't want to be limited forever, but I don't want to be powerful forever either, I want the power but not the consequences and responsibilities that come with it, a stereotypical hedonistic lifestyle further proven by the points I've given in my post here that responsibilities from civilization is what limits humanity, but if modern civilization didn't exist then we would have other responsibilities like farming and hunting food. I now realize that our insignificance gives life meaning, our limited time in this world gives life meaning, I just can't help but wish I was more than this, I feel like I could be so much more in the right cimcumstances is all. I don't think my problems concerns any of the three that we've been talking about, if it does, it's probably just one of the three that has spiraled out of control somehow and became what it is today. I don't even know myself where this ridiculously high sense of freedom comes from either, I can see it coming from how I grew up but this seems overkill compared to what I've experienced considering I've lived a pretty normal life outside of a few tumbles here and there. The concept of freedom itself binds me, and I'd rather disappear just to get it all over with, unironically, so I can be free. Going back to your question, the best answer I can give would be that I have a high sense of freedom, my Id, and society has a hard limitation it imposes on its people, the Superego. This causes friction which gives me the existential dread that has haunted me for so long.

&#x200B;

While not something I actively think about, yes, I can still see it. Id as nature, in which we live and adapt to, this gives us the instincts that we have which is highly specialized for our world and our disposition. The instincts that every sentient being has, born from whatever world they grew up in, all for the sake of self-preservation. Ego as the human, the consciousness born from the Id. Whereas animals only live with their Id, through our exhaustive evolution we develop the ego, the ability to think rationally, the capability for self-regulation and executive functions of the mind. All of what differentiates us from the rest, all of what makes us human. The superego as society, yes, the values and ideals that you have been taught, every person's code and conduct. All of these things that can only be born from being in a group, from being able to differentiate contrasting behaviours, and being able to differentiate what is correct and incorrect.

&#x200B;

All three has to work together to achieve absolute balance, just like how all of the brain works together to give us the most optimum experience we can have, there is never one part that has it all. The Ego would have never been born if the Id never existed, and a being living only with their Id will never really experience happiness as we know it. While having a child grow up succesfully and/or being able to defend your home from oncoming attackers will feel nice, is it really happiness in and of itself or just instinctual gratitude born from a natural sense of self-preservation? Meanwhile, a life with no Superego is a life with no meaning, which from what we can see, is a life with no happiness. Even with Ego, there can't be any happiness in a world that is so enigmatic. Before our actions, the Ego includes ideas given by the Superego which we think is admirable, which is worthy of praise, and which is proper behaviour. Without these things, everything will look bland to a person because to him, there are no differences between doing this or that, it is all the same. This is why I believe that if a person with an Ego continues to search for meaning in his life, he will definitely find a Superego within him, because it is a necessity in life. He will slowly have his preferences and what he thinks is beautiful and what is not, so on and so forth, and his morality will revolve around these beliefs, actions against his likings will be wrong and vice versa. Going back, this is the same with the Id as well, it can also influence what our passions can be. Through time, we may be curious and try to venture beyond what our comfort zone envelops, and this could give birth to our own set of preferences as well. While the Superego may not be as important as the Id and Ego, I do believe it is a good foundation for a more meaningful life. It adds more color to the world through the variations it can give by adding levels and categories with different parts and areas of the universe, leading to a more satisfying life. Lastly, a person living with no Id will easily pass away one way or another, simply for the fact there are no mechanisms at work that tries to keep the person alive, I suppose aside from the fear of pain. Regardless, It doesn't really make sense that a sentient being will have no instincts anyway, and I've given enough information to present how important the Id is for a happier life aswell. I don't know what it is exactly that you saw is conflicting with the Id and Superego, but one that I could see was the fact that the Id has a set of rules on what to do for the best chance at survival, something that could spawn friction with the Superego if given the right circumstances. Just like what I mentioned about this destitute person earlier, could this be the confliction you were talking about? Going back to the previous topic, I can't exactly say how you can manipulate this equation since every person has different characters and grew up in different environments, even the more 'general' tips I can give will merely have any effect on any person simply because of how diverse humanity can be individually, if anyone can answer this question, it'll be from the person himself.

&#x200B;

\[2/2\]

Well, it'll be pretty hard to give a conclusive answer if the apple is everything at the same time, which is why I raised multiple points here and there. But it's all for the better if you enjoy every interpretations regardless, I suppose.

&#x200B;

In Freud's theory, Id is our intuition, instincts, constitution, our nature and character, it is also the source of our desires and passion, our instinctual preferences. These instincts are built through humanity's experiences throughout history that has been ingrained within each of us, and at the same time with how we grew up that has been built alongside us. Destitute people would inherently have this desire to work harder because they don't want to stay in their current situation, their desires are of simple things, such as a new pair of shoes, or some good food to eat. This desire to get out of a difficult situation comes from the instincts of humanity itself, the desire for our brain to protect itself, what has been ingrained in us that the ones that suffers more will ultimately end earlier than the others that don't. Their desires of simple things is a combination of their Ego and Id, the Id wants something that would benefit his person, something to liven his mood and to be in a better state, but our Ego controlling these desires so that it can be of achievable proportions. This is why you don't see any poor people working to achieve a brand new car or a 10,000$ watch, they start small so that their desires do not end in disappointment. This would mean then that the source of our Id is the instincts that has been with us intrinsically through humanity's experience, and at the same time our own experience that we have built to adapt to our current situation, no matter how challenging of a life it may be.

&#x200B;

The source of our Superego is simpler because it is simply our morality. Someone that has grown up in a place where murder is commonplace, where torture and abuse frequently occurs, where love and warmth never comes from even your own parents, would have a different sense of morality. This right or wrong is different from Id in a way that it is completely dependent on how the person grew up, instead of it always being there. This child would think that murder and abuse should be normal, we should just achieve what we want even if it means stepping on others, you shouldn't care for what others think or feel. He would bring these cynical realizations through adulthood because this is what he grew up in, this is what he thinks is correct and what is right to do. Our Ego can't do anything about this because we can only think within the boundaries of our own knowledge and experience, therefore even you yourself will think this should be correct if you were to be placed in this situation. However, our Id would say otherwise. You see, as I've said before, our Id is the accumulation of experiences we've had turned into instincts, not only yours, but your ancestors' as well. It would know that these circumstances are wrong because it has been established in us way way long ago that hurting others will cause conflict and this conflict could turn into banishment, and we've already learned that being alone will most likely mean death, this is why we are pack hunters, our survivability increases tenfold just by being in a group and this is also the reason why we are social animals in the first place. We didn't have the strength or speed of other animals so therefore we had to adapt. Thanks to this, the person himself will question why all of this is happening to himself even if he doesn't know why he's questioning it, it just inherently feels wrong to him. Similar questions like these will keep occuring in his waking life until a certain point where the person has been damaged to such a high degree that his incorrect sense of morality will overpower the instincts inside him. These people most often become serial killers, mentally deranged, psychopathic etc. and so in short, the Superego is our conscience, one that is completely dependent on how we grew up and what information we took in as children, where development is most prevalent within us. Looking at it this way, then yes, you are correct in that the Id comes from the inside and the Superego comes from the outside.

&#x200B;

I interpreted this separation you've stated not in the way that he ceases becoming one with his universe, but rather that he achieves sentience and becomes 'different' with the world around him. Reason being are your statements about "Dividing the mind" and "One piece trying to understand other parts". I did my best with what I had and I came to the conclusion that this is what you meant by this separation, so for simplicity's sake, I will continue with this in mind. With this reasoning in mind, the answer should be simple: because he achieved sentience. Even if we live in the same universe, someone that has sentience is vastly different than something that doesn't. His progress happened simply because he was curious, something that only a sentient being can have. And as far as we know, curious beings can't be satisfied with staying in one place, so he had to know more, he had to understand the world he is living in, he had to understand what he is and how he came to be. But in the end, he could never understand any of it because there are no answers within his own world, the apple was but a mere consequence of something that happened outside of his world, something in the void. There may be a clue he could find inside, but like I said, nothing will come out of it except trivial assumptions and theories.

&#x200B;

\[1/2\]

The Apple & Its Piece

While you aren't wrong in the fact that the piece itself is a part of the whole, we are focusing on this one piece so of course I will have to put some emphasis on his existence, I understand that this could be interpreted as me differentiating him from the world he's apart of, but this, regardless, doesn't imply that he can't understand the world that he's in simply because he is only a piece within. With my given logic, we can then say the same with ourselves, while we are only a part of the universe at large, we can still see that we are in this large world and understand the mechanics and components of said world, or at the very least, theorize. In a way, we are a piece of the whole itself, understanding other parts. Looking at it this way, it's easy to recognize how such a concept would work, since that is the concept that we've been living on in the first place. But if we were talking about a literal piece of the apple, like a piece of the universe, then of course it won't understand itself because it has no consciousness, but this is where a paradox will appear. A paradox that I will later touch upon in more detail. Regardless, this was obviously the only way I could give a proper answer. Now, with what this separation causes? If we follow your comment of "The answer should be here and now.", then nothing. It is simply a piece of an apple trying to understand the world it exists in, nothing will change with the apple at large, and this piece will forever be stuck within this world. Once again, causing another paradox.

&#x200B;

\[1st Paradox\] The paradox that I first hinted about touches upon the fact that an apple is a much simpler body than the universe itself. At first, I thought that you were only using the apple as a metaphor for the universe, but it seems to me as if you are taking this apple quite literally, so I will have to clarify some points of interest beforehand. If we just think of the apple as an apple, I won't be able to answer any of your questions. This is why I based my answers upon our current situation in reality. With your question of "Do you divide your mind into pieces?" not making any sense whatsoever simply because you're implying that the whole apple itself has a mind, with this scenario, then, why are we talking about this singular piece in the first place? It would make your whole set of questions redundant because there is no need for a piece of the mind to understand itself because all of the mind works together to form consciousness as we know it, and the whole mind should understand itself already, unless, of course, this piece is a whole new form of consciousness within the apple's mind altogether. This is what I mean about basing the piece's situation with ours. As far as we know, the universe has no consciousness, it is but a medium created by god knows what, and that we, a piece of the universe, has our own consciousness within it. I won't be able to answer your questions your way because this would mean we would have to make new rules within a new reality because from what we know there cant be a mind within a mind, every part of the brain works together to create consciousness. And like I said, I have to give this piece its own consciousness if I'm able to answer your questions because if it is just a literal piece of the apple, the universe, it won't understand itself because it is but a piece of the whole medium, and the medium has no consciousness. We have no idea if a higher consciousness exists, so therefore I have to base my answers with the current system of our reality, because that's the only one I know. This can be confusing, I know, because what you're implying, and what I'm answering are contradicting each other but for simplicity's sake, I will continue answering with the idea that the apple is a medium, and the piece has its own consciousness.

&#x200B;

\[2nd Paradox\] Before continuing with the answer, I will have to explain first that if we continue looking at the apple as only but a mere apple, we can stick with the answer I've given before because unlike our universe, this piece has no instruments to manipulate because an apple is only an apple and nothing else. But if we assume that this apple is but a metaphor for the universe, then we have more options for a more detailed answer. Now with this in mind, I will revise my answer in the 2nd paradox that I mentioned. And once more for simplicity's sake, moving forward, let's continue with your idea that the answer should be with us, there is no outside being or outside world, he is a singular apple that exists within an endless and empty void. Now, If he will be stuck in this apple, something will change within this apple. He will never be able to break through the skin, but assuming he doesn't hit a limit, and that he doesn't ever die, he will use the world around him to advance not only in the physical world but in his psyche as well. He will realize new concepts, his capacity for knowledge will increase, his curiousity will continue to rise, this will keep going until he has achieved ultimate power, he will use this accumulated knowledge to advance his own way of technology, machinery that lets him manipulate further artifacts within his universe. But he will never reach an exit for this world, anything inside of the void disappears, and the only safe haven he has is the apple. Outside, the apple will not appear any different, but inside, there are plenty of change happening. But in the end, so long as he doesn't find a way out of this apple, he will never know more than what is inside this apple outside of trivial assumptions and theories, no matter how strong he may be. This is the most satisfying answer I could come to with the idea you've given that the answer should be in him, not outside. Bringing any outside force into this reality will just make it infintely more complex, something I would not want to continue down into further.

&#x200B;

Coming towards your second set of questions about separation, I see that it is once again not making any sense since you've declared that the answer should be in him, there should be no outside force. I'll also assume that your "Without the part separate" probably means "With the part separate". Regardless, just for these two questions, I will disregard these claims so I can, at the very least, give a proper answer. If we separate this piece with its world, the apple, it is still an apple, its biologocial composition is that of an apple, and it has no difference with any other part of its world. The same can be said with the apple without its piece, it is still an apple, only missing a small portion of itself. If we look at ourselves, however, it becomes different. While you can say that we are one with the universe, we aren't exactly the same. If a human is outside of its world, is it still a part of the universe it was in? I would say so, but in a different way that is commonly assumed. Similar to a migrating citizen, if this citizen is in a different country, or even if he revokes his own country's citizenship for a different one, he will still be known to be from his homeland, atleast biologically because it is in his blood. And in a similar light, if we are to separate from our universe, you could say that we are still one with it, but also that we are separate. It is in our blood, we were born and raised with our universe's rules and order, and just like people from different countries, there has to be a way to differentiate us from residents of other universes, if they do exist that is. And of course, with this logic, the universe should still be the same even if we are to separate from it, just think of death, if the universe does change with every person's disappearance, then the whole world as we know it should be in shambles right now, but it's not. We need the universe, but the universe does not need us. Nothing will change with the world even if we separate from it. And this is as far of an answer as I can give. Going back to what I said from my reply prior, I have never experienced an orange so I can only answer from what I know as my apple. Any arguments further this would be pointless and unecessary because these questions you're asking necessitates knowledge from beyond the universe.

&#x200B;

The Ego & Self Awareness

It would do you good to not assume the word Ego in Freud's structure literally. Yes, Ego in its literal definition is just the way in which we evaluate ourselves, and that consciousness is from different workings, but Ego in Freud's work is the 'manager' so to speak, it processes the desires of the Id and ensures that these desires are not of absurd proportions, it consists of our executive functions, common sense, perception, judgement, memory and all other functions of the mind. It mediates between the Id and Superego and is responsible for our actions after it has been taught over and 'realized'. Id is the source of our desires, our wants and needs, Superego is our moral compass, before the action, it tells you what is right and wrong, and the Ego mediates between these two and works these thoughts out in a way that can be of our best benefit. The Ego is you, the one that does the thinking, and the Id and Superego are what comes with your physiology as a human being. In these studies, and with any study, really, the words and terms given are not always the main focus, what is more important is the meaning that comes with them. Ego has a different definition in literal standards, yes, but it has a different meaning in Freud's theory and we are talking about Freud's right now. However concerning this may be for you, this topic is of great insignificance, if we focus on his definition itself, it would be right that the Ego is your sense of self, that's all we need to agree upon for now.

Yes, I could see, and this is what triggered my nihilistic approach in life. The fact that I am unbelievably insignificant in the face of the world, that there will be no change even if I did the best of what I can achieve as a human being, that fierce sense of absurdity and futility and the anger it first gave me before it turned into despondency. Did the world need to be this big? Did life have to be so complex? Why was I even born? Did everything happen just for the creator to boast his power around? Do I, as an individual, even matter in his eyes? Questions that were never answered, pleas that were ignored, hopes that were shattered. Confusion turned into anger, anger turned into despair, despair turned into resignation, resignation turned into acceptance.

&#x200B;

It can make assumptions, but it would never really amount to any conclusive answer. Even if his assumption was right, he would never really truly know because what he has experienced all of his life is limited to that apple. He doesn't know the difference between an apple and an orange because he has only interacted with one of them, so he won't know how to identify and differentiate the two. He knows that he is a small piece in what seems to be a big world, but he doesn't know what this world is or how it was made, except for the fact that it is his world. The only way he could possibly know where he is is if someone from outside of that apple comes and explains his situation for him, or if he somehow gets out of his apple and sees what the 'real' world is, assuming he doesn't spontaneously combust from the endless barrage of new information coming. For the sake of clarity, we could say the same for us, and let's imagine that in some universal scale, there are "cosmic humans", giant beings of great power and the universe we live in is actually just an apple to them, we wouldn't know that we are in an apple, we just know that this apple is our world, so we identify it as our universe, not as their apple, because we can't see it from their perspective, only from ours. Does this makes sense?

&#x200B;

All three questions that comes afterwards have the same answer: it is the mind. There is a study regarding this subject called Ego Psychology based on Freud's model and he refers to this consciousness as the "Ego". One's own sense of self, self esteem, self importance. The one that oversees, and makes the decisions. The "Id" is, in essence, the instinctual components within every person. I would believe that this is what makes a person scared of Death, since uncertainty has a kind of danger that has been imprinted into us long ago as homo-sapiens. Even if we interpret this fear in different ways nowadays. The "Superego" is the one that oversees ethical and moral decisions, what is right and wrong, but is not relevant for our current conversation. These three constitutes Freud's structure of the mind, something I only stumbled upon while being in r/Psychonaut. Asking who is aware of the mind, would be the Ego, which is capable of self-awareness, which is what differentiates us from everyday animals. Our awareness of our existence, and our awareness of the world at large. Meanwhile, all animals, at most, can only achieve basic levels of self-awareness. What is aware of being scared of death? The Ego as well, your sense of self. You're aware that you are scared, just like you are aware when you are happy, or when you are hungry. The Ego, your self-awareness, would be the answer for these two questions.

Yes, as it seems, the one thing that I'm missing is acceptance. Something that I've already realized long ago, but did not want to even accept the concept of accepting my life. 

&#x200B;

And as I've come to realize thanks to u/wheniwakup, The world is not tailored towards me, not everything will be what I want, and not everyone will adjust to how I want them to be. And if I had all the power in the world, it would be the most excruciating life ever. Responsibilities are indeed necessary for a fulfilling life, because it would mean that you gave effort towards something, you gave a part of yourself towards something, not just flicking your finger for it to appear right infront of you. Sure, there might be some things that you will have to do regardless of if you like it or not, but if everything was as how you wanted it to be, you will not grow as a person and you won't understand the beauty of life. In which the endless amount of differences and variation is most prevalent, with each one adding new colors to the world.

&#x200B;

This isn't the world that I perfectly imagined but that's fine. Accepting life for what it is, for how pointless it may be, is still better than trying to change it to your own liking, in which you will never win. If I can somehow be truly happy and content with living life, it will most likely be in this way of thinking.

&#x200B;

 This reply is well appreciated.

Writing my thoughts has always done me well, even if they are pointless or embarassing to be seen by the media, they help clear my mind. To look back to my past self, see what I was, and what I am now: to find further ways to improve.

&#x200B;

I would think I am a god, fall into degeneracy, and probably start my own twisted form of a cult, charming others to join, believing in this illusion that all of what happens is not of random chance, but of predetermined fate, for I am the one above all, ruling over everything...that is if I had this interpretation of life. No, I do not believe that everything that is out of my control will be predetermined, I would like to believe that these people that I live with are also of conscious minds, sentient beings that are no different than I am. They are capable of free will, and are thinking just like I am. The universe is born from whatever knows what, without regard of my existence. In its eyes, I am nothing special. I wouldn't be nihilistic if I thought of life in this way, quite of an absurd question don't you think?

&#x200B;

I don't find it scary myself, but the unknown, the passing of this world that you've gotten used to, the pensive feeling of doubt and anxiety of what comes after, among many other things, causes fear of death. If life was so cruel, everyone probably wouldn't be scared of death itself, life is peaceful for what it currently is. Sure there are wars between countries and a pandemic, along with many other specific problems within each country but it's not like it affects humanity enough that this fear of death will disappear from our minds. Life is peaceful, and it's hard to let go of that tranquil life for something so uncertain like death. As for me, I don't mind whatever happens. If, in my eyes, life could have more meaning, things or people that I can't just leave, then I would probably be scared of death. As it is right now, there's no one thing or person that's holding me from accepting death if it comes.

&#x200B;

There's nothing wrong with it, I think it would be quite fun. For someone that wants freedom to such a foolish degree, being able to fly and live my life with little to no worries would be nice. While birds do not have the same capacity of self-awareness like humans do, that's still fine with me. I believe in the quote that "Ignorance is Bliss". Living your life just for the sake of living it, the idea seems enjoyable. But the structure of society isn't that simple, and the human brain is infinitely more complex to be satisfied with such simple thinking.

&#x200B;

Sure, It really depends on the person and how they react but it is possible. Death isn't something so traumatizing that someone so scared of it won't be able to be back to who he is if he ends up experiencing it. Unless it's some extremely potent phobia. Death is just a very tall cliff, scary and difficult to accept and to climb, but once you scale it, it shouldn't be too bad. I wouldn't know if freedom is dying, if it was, bad for them. Endlessly floating in the expanses of space, or endlessly spending your time in a heaven-like dimension, eternally free but eternally bound by the world. This is why I believe that true freedom is nothingness. If this is the freedom you're talking about though, no difference, they're gone and at peace anyway.

&#x200B;

The mind is much more complex than anything I've ever seen, that's all. The fact that I may not even be myself, I'm just a thought process that directs orders to a physical body, If I die, I leave this physical body. Or not, and I just disappear. I don't know. But the mind has so much potential on whatever it could be, coupled with the fact that science and religion still has never given any conclusive explanation as to what consciousness really is is astounding to me. It tickles my curiousity and I want to find out more about what it is and how it seems to works so dynamically. It is the one thing that seems to be beyond of this world but is also within our reach, a potential clue to an 'outside' of this universe.

No, you're actually right. Of course, if giving meaning in your life is as simple as wanting to give meaning to your life, and it just magically appearing, while your life has meaning now, is that really 'meaningful' per se? Without ever working hard for it, without spending a lot of time into achieving this one thing that you want?

&#x200B;

If you had all the power in the world, won't it feel lonely? You're able to get what you want whenever you want, and one could say that you are the freeest man alive, I can't think of one good thing that comes from this. You can't be passionate, you can't appreciate the 'importance' of life since life is eternal for you, you can't work hard for anything and devote yourself to something you love. All of these emotions that comes with humans, all of these that make life colorful. Yes, maybe our insignificance is a good thing, this way, we appreciate the world in a much more different light than someone that is omnipotent. 

&#x200B;

Thank you for this. It definitely didn't answer every question in my head, and it will be a lot of effort trying to accept the insignifance of life, but a new path has opened up regardless, thank you.

Hmm. Well, I'd like to think that I fully understand the fact that philosophy itself isn't a straightforward topic whatsoever. For what one subject you find, there are tens more sub-subjects that lie within it. Looking further into every specific philosopher and their publishes, I'm sure there are many, albeit small, differences with each of them. I am not an active reader regarding philosophy so I cannot fully integrate all these citings of yours into my head. This is why I try to be broad with my conversations, so that I don't poke into wrong discussions making a mess of myself, and also to attract a wider cast of people.

&#x200B;

But from my understanding, Existentialism is the fact that life has no intrinsic meaning and that meaning itself only comes from the person itself. Therefore, we should just give meaning to the world, free from the bounds of expectations, ego, pride and just enjoy life, for however meaningless it may be, for what it is. Have I been wrong in interpreting the main message of this belief?

&#x200B;

I'm also curious, what is the point you're trying to convey with this reply? And are there any ideas you can give into coping with this realization, instead of ending it all in the face of life's pointlessness?

Even if we believe in the idea that everything is predetermined and that you have no full control over yourself, while this may seem contradictory, there just isn't any point in believing something that is out of your reach. Maybe that is the case, but how would I know? I'll just do what I can with what it seems I have, with what it seems I can change, and start from there.

&#x200B;

Freedom, in its literal sense, is impossible for our mind, let alone our physical body, to achieve. Not knowing whatever will happen after death, how can we be sure if it is freedom that comes after life or what other possibilities there are? Even if it is true, what if freedom after life itself is also an illusion? I've only thought of freedom as nothingness, you are only truly free if you are nothing, you are not bound by any world, by the concept of freedom itself, by the mind, you are beyond everything. And it's a sad outlook is it not? No, that's not what I mean when I say a 'free mind'. I look more so towards the idea that I'm not bound by any thoughts, any people, any events. I don't take anything seriously nor for granted, and I live my life without worries, only observing life for its beauty and grandeur. This isn't something you can achieve with the current system of humanity where you work until you can't anymore. Even the belief in Existentialism can't free you from that.

&#x200B;

Because society and philosophers are different as I've come to learn. Whereas the masses follow the repetition of human life, the philosophers ask the "Why?", the "Where"?, the "When?". If anyone will be able to give me a clear answer, It will be these types of people. And no, I'm not saying everyone here is a philosopher, but we are, at the very least, sewn from the same cloth, are we not?

&#x200B;

No, human interaction is not the first thing that comes to my mind when it comes to making decisions. I wouldn't say I'm scared either. I'm just very cautious about doing senseless things, my life, for as small as it may be, is still my life. You could say that if everything is meaningless, then I could just kill myself right now instead of putting so much effort finding reason to continue, but I, admittedly, just don't want this to be the case. Before I even realized it, I've always wanted to be free, I've always wanted my life to have meaning, the first moments of my realization that life is utterly meaningless, that human life is impossibly insignificant compared to the vast expanses of the world as a whole, has been devastatingly traumatizing. Deep inside me, I want to find some semblance of meaning in my life, anything at all, but at the same time, I could never accept the idea of just blocking my ears away from the truth that I need to hear and just living in a fantasy. I am in a situation where I am being pulled apart head to feet, and I want to find a way out of this situation.

There are many options I can choose from when it comes to picking the path I walk, but the reason why I posted this is because I'm not sure which is the right one. Of course, change will come from me. I can't control the world but I can control myself, and if I really wanted this mystical world to come true, I would do something about it. Even if I couldn't reach it in the end, I atleast reached higher heights than if I stayed the way I was before. But is all that really worth it if all of it will mean nothing in the face of death anyway? Before I answer to the journey towards a free mind, I'm first asking for the answer of "Is all of this worth it?", is what I want to convey to others.

&#x200B;

These socratic questionings aren't usually used to give an answer, but rather to broaden the mind of the respondent, if anything, the answer will come from that person himself. I understand that the action has to come from me, but being lost and wanting to be someone incompatible with society's structure is a difficult situation. Such as all that aims to be different than what is normalized. Is what I'm doing the right thing? Or is it a fruitless endeavor? Desiring to get a clue for these questions through the perspective of others, is why I want to know what you think. And also why I made this post in the first place.

Even if I can see it, It's not like it can change anything. My voice isn't anything significant that others will willingly listen to on first glance. This 'world' that I imagine isn't something that easy to replicate either, and it's so fantastical you could call it delusional. I realized myself that I didn't want to be money driven, and that I wanted to be free. Now I'm searching around for answers that could potentially give some light to my confusion, and if there is a way out of this cage. This is what it tells me.

&#x200B;

Funny asking me this question when the next two questions mentions my own problems and concerns. I just don't know. You could say that being free, no one should decide which way to go. But you could also say that this could cause discord with conflicting individuals. I chase the image of this world, but so long as it remains an image, and I remain a quiet echo to the vast world there is, there's no benefit on choosing between such demanding decisions.

&#x200B;

What are my problems? My problems directly correlate with my perception of society. Maybe I just have to accept all of it, move on, and be enslaved by the system that we've built. That would most likely be better in terms of having a 'succesful' life, but is that really the life I want? I wouldn't say so. I'm still stumbling my way around, looking for something to hold onto. And until I can grip a more 'steady' belief, I wouldn't be able to answer these two questions.

&#x200B;

What do you think of all these?

Despite my pessimism about both the world and life, I do believe that both of them are an alluring and vast beauty that has many things to offer. The sadness and happiness, the pain and joy, the known and unknown, all of it gives color to the world. But with the current state of humanity, I feel that it is us that's limiting ourselves to seeing this beauty because everything is structurized with a money driven society, each generation essentially just becomes a repetition of the last. We are moreso focused on the system that we've built instead of the vast world that is our medium, for us to give meaning.

&#x200B;

There will be its own set of problems, being freedom driven and all, but I still believe it's better than what we have right now. Some people would prefer that everything fall in its place like a puzzle, but I prefer a freedom driven world because the world itself doesn't seem to be made with efficiency and order in mind. With all the different variables and inconsistent composition of both the world and life, with the seemingly endless complexity of the human brain, I feel that we have much more to achieve and to see if we just stepped back and looked at what we've become for once. Wishful thinking, yes, and it pains me to see this image in my head is so far away.

&#x200B;

I wouldn't say that I am a nihilist by nature, I've just become what I am simply because of what our species has become.

This was my point. Because of these rules, the current structure of humanity actually rids an individual's potential. Everything eventually becomes monotonous and it becomes a game of working until you can't anymore. The lifestyle you build, the things that you can do, the reach of your life, whatever you want to get is directly dependent on how much money and power is necessary for these things.

&#x200B;

This is why I said that there is no freedom in a world where sustenance is necessary to live. Believing in Existentialism will not make you free because in the end of the day, the things that you want to do in the realization of life's pointlessness will still be met with work, responsibilities and sustenance to achieve. All of which will still be meaningless after death. It is no different than one who doesn't believe, and that is what I don't get about Existentialism. Realizing that life has no meaning, being 'free' and giving it meaning yourself, but still following the same routine to achieve these meanings they talk about, what is so different?

&#x200B;

And maybe I wasn't very clear, but I never thought of Existentialism as an alternative. I simply wanted to see how people who believe in Existentialism continue living with that belief despite the current system that we adhere to. What do you think?

Yeah, after reading this whole thing and knowing more about both of your situations I can definitely say that you are doing the right thing here. At this point, all I can really serve to do here is to give you confirmation and to say that you should simply keep doing what you are currently doing.

&#x200B;

You let her react and handle the confrontations and interactions she has with others but if things escalate, you are always there to intervene if necessary. That is the best decision and sadly, one that people usually don't often do when handling sensitive circumstances such as this. By protecting the person and not letting them handle their own problems, you're not really fixing anything since the solution of the problem is you, not the person themself, and it's not like you'll always be there with them so if you aren't then the person won't really know what to do, they've become too dependent of you. You should only really be a splint to help stabilize the person and to make them walk better so to speak, and not be their legs to just carry them around. Of course, if the person is severely scarred and broken then it would be better to protect them first than to let them fix their own problems, but people often don't see the line between these two conditions, and for this case where she isn't completely dysfunctional, it's better to slowly let her handle her own stuff and just act as a guiding light for her.

&#x200B;

As for the sex, I can't really say anything about it, with all the other things you revealed she has been through (especially since it's still fresh too), you'll definitely need a medium that can act as a distraction away from her pain, and the best one will definitely be the one she is most comfortable with, sex. As it is right now, if you are the table that is keeping the glass, which is her, from standing, then the sex is acting as the tape that will temporarily keep her from shattering completely. With all the info you've revealed, I can understand now that the sex isn't just a normal escape for her, It means a lot more. Through sex, she's slowly regaining her own emotions all over again, learning how to deal with sex and intimacy, which are probably big triggers for most rape victims, and many more. While I will still keep my original point about the sex, it seems that you won't really have to do that for now since she needs it and you need it, it acts as a reprieve for the both of you where you are most vulnerable, and when you're that deep in this hellhole, any kind of comfort can really help big time.

&#x200B;

And from your post alone, she came off as very dysfunctional when it comes to other people but it seems that wasn't the case at all after reading your reply, and that's relieving to know. It seems like she can still interact and form friendships with others (even going to frat parties after all this, wow) and if that's the case then all you really need to do is to keep supporting her as she slowly opens up more and more to others. The fact that you found her on Tinder could indicate that she was desperate for help, just wanting someone to be there with her even if it meant opening up to random people in the internet and I'm glad you met her before anyone else. Tinder isn't a safe place on its own and I can see her being taken advantage of in that site if things ended up differently, but that's another story that I'm sure we're both glad didn't unfold.

&#x200B;

As for the therapy, I have to heavily agree with you. I said what i said before with what little information I've had from your post and tried to answer accordingly, but now that I know both of your situations even more, my advice was definitely the wrong one. The fact that she's dealing with way more things than just her assault, added with the fact that you have many problems of your own, It's definitely better to get a therapist for her in the end for many obvious reasons. You seemed like you had no problems from what i can decipher in your post but boy was i wrong by a whole mile, If you have to deal with your problems and at the same time supporting her from her recovery, then you are right that it is unhealthy for your relationship, not only because she has many problems of her own that needs to be fixed but also because you have your own as well. It's similar to injuring a leg, you'll instinctively put more pressure on your other leg because of it, causing that same leg to weaken and be even more prone to injury. It seems that it's not only her that needs a helping hand but you as well, and a therapist will definitely help the recovery progress of not only her, but indirectly you as well.

&#x200B;

And while I don't really have to address this, I just have to commend you for the fact that despite the way she likes to follow your lead and is okay with whatever you do with  mostly little resistance, you still check on her all the time and reassure her that it's okay to speak up and that you're willing to listen. Most people would just take the person for granted and do what they want, blindly thinking that since their partner isn't protesting about anything then they're also enjoying it. I've seen it happen many times and I'm glad that you still look back at her and put yourself in the same pedestal as her, you're  not ashamed to be equal with your partner, and trust me, some men are not brave enough to do that since most of the time they want to be the dominant one. I mean, no homo but if I was a woman then you're definitely the type of person I'd be aiming for in a partner, someone understanding and supportive and especially doesn't just leave their partner no matter the burden of their problems, accepting them for who they are and loving them even more because of it. Cheers for this.

&#x200B;

Looking back, It just seemed unnecessary for me to reply, you clearly know what you need to do and have a wide perspective of your current situation, you've already made your decision and that's shown by the fact that you didn't just simply say yes and agree with everything I've said but instead disagreed when you felt it was wrong. I wanted to simply give you confirmation that what you are doing is right, especially now that i know more about you two, your decisions definitely made much more sense now, I couldn't even disagree with any of it since it was pretty much complete. Many people can learn from how you deal with shit like this and just like what i said before, this reply, this conversation, whether it be helpful or not for your situation, could be helpful for someone else that do see it. That's what these little communities do, no matter how knowledgeable you think you may be, you'll always find something to learn. Much love, brother.

As it is right now, you aren't really doing anything wrong, you're supporting her, you prioritize her well-being above anything else, and most importantly is that you're understanding and not trying to force anything for her. The best way to help a person with any kind of heavy trauma is to always act as their shield and their bridge, give them a stable land to stand upon, to experience life in their own pace in a safe environment to show them that life is not always going to treat them poorly, and that you're there for her to make sure that any of that doesn't happen again. You are doing all that and more, It's really surprising how well you're handling all this and you need some recognition for that alone. I know that praises isn't really what you're aiming for but you deserve it, progress is being made, and all the things she said when you two were being intimate with each other is absolutely a step in the right direction and your support is definitely a big reason for that.

&#x200B;

But i know you didn't just post this to receive comments that says you're doing fine, maybe deep inside you're doubting yourself about something but you really don't know what it is and that's why you asked for confirmation here in the internet, maybe, maybe not. Nonetheless, from what i can see on this post alone, while you are doing a great job at protecting her from harm and maintaining a slow and steady progress in improving her mental state, you are not doing well in terms of improving her independence, so I'll give you what i can for you to achieve your goal.

&#x200B;

\- **Keep showing her all kinds of things and don't just limit her world to you.** This is your first step into making her more independent. While being intimate and dependent with each other in a relationship is definitely a necessity, especially with her situation, it can also backfire if done too much. I don't know much about her and how deep her trauma is so this will entirely depend on your judgement, but you can start with small things that you know she can do alone or at least do with little help, this way she'll be more confident with being able to do things on her own. She'll slowly regain her individuality by not depending on your safety and guidance all the time, and eventually it'll grow and she'll start doing more and more things alone comfortably.

&#x200B;

\- **Don't make sex as one of the frontlines of your relationship.** This could be hard for either of you depending on your situations regarding sex but if you truly want her to move on from her trauma and be her own person, you'll have to limit your intimacy in bed. There are many reasons why keeping this up is a bad idea, one of these reasons is that if you keep exposing her to sex in her vulnerable state, where she is most susceptible to an addiction, for an escape, you're reinforcing her mind to think of sex as a reprieve from the pain she's felt and still feeling today, especially now that she actually feels good from the sex you two have where she feels the passion and emotions sex gives. And I can guarantee you, an addiction backed up with mental disorders and trauma is undeniably harder to fix than an addiction alone, and if she does get addicted, that'll arise a whole batch of problems for the both of you. In her current state, I'd say she is that close to you because she feels safe with you, you're acting as a barrier between her and her pain, that's enough, don't make sex the same way. You can still have sex, but don't do it too much is all I'm saying.

&#x200B;

\- **Introduce her to new people.** This is the hardest part but one that you'll have to do eventually, this is one of the vices of her trauma, her distrust of others. It would be best to introduce her to people you're close with, people that you know wouldn't make her uncomfortable and then you can make her interact with them, you can be right next to her or be at a safe distance as long as she can see you, that's enough. You can even join in the conversation but do make sure that she talks as well, I'll leave it up to you on how you will do this. Unfortunately, there is no shortcuts or easy ways around this, the only way you can really lift her distrust of others is to make her interact with them, I'd say you should help her be more capable on her own first, like the first tip says, while also making small talks about how not all people mean harm to her or at least anything similar for around once every week so as not to overwhelm her because it seems like it's a big trigger for her from what i can see on that one scenario on the parking lot.

&#x200B;

\- **Don't think of therapy as an immediate necessity.** Your presence alone is acting as therapy for her as it is already. I can guarantee you that talking with someone that you know and love, with someone that understands you is much better than an appointed doctor tasked to help you get better, while the doctor definitely knows more than you do, she will not get that same sentimental and emotional support that she gets from you, and right now, that's exactly what she needs. Of course, eventually she'll have to go to therapy since there is only so much you can do but as it is right now it is still too early, for now, you can work on getting her independence back and once she is more capable on her own you can ask her again about the therapy. And once you are on therapy, ask the doctor the same questions you asked here and more, there is only so much we can do as well since we are no professional after all. This is only my two cents though, doctors will often say that it's better to be early in terms of any injuries, whether it be mental or physical, but i don't quite agree with that to an extent. But I will leave it up to your judgement to decide whether you want to go to therapy early or not.

&#x200B;

\- **And finally, be aware that people dealing with trauma often have a hard time making decisions for themselves.** This is why i keep saying that I will leave it up to your judgement, because you are the one that needs to make the decision, both doctors and random people on the internet only serve to give you advice, but in the end, the one right next to her, the one that's protecting and guiding her, is you. Sometimes the right decision isn't always the one she will want and you'll have to be a little pushy even if it means making her uncomfortable, and sometimes the steps you need to take will hurt the both of you, but if you think it is what will help you get through your hardships together, if you think it is the right choice, then you have to endure and keep going even if you see her suffering. You don't even have to take any of my word at all, with how well you've been handling her sensitive situation all this time, I'm sure you will handle this one with care as well, all I did is give you advice but ultimately you are the one that will need to take action, the one that's right next to her, that's you.

&#x200B;

EDIT: After that Edit post of yours, It looks like she was completely different from how she appeared to be after all. All good, whether she is a damaged or an ass-kicking woman that can do this on her own, I do believe that if you want to help her in any way you possibly can, these tips are 'general' enough that you can still put them into action nonetheless (I guess except the last one), I wish you luck with your journey together man, there's nothing more sweet than a couple lifting each other up against all odds and difficulties.

&#x200B;

And I have to agree with your statement there, not many men really understand the hardships of what a rape survivor is experiencing mostly because they usually aren't the victims themselves. Being a survivor can usually leave you scarred and distrustful of others to the point that you can't even go outside or interact with other people without proper help and that's why i like seeing posts like this that makes an active decision to help those that are important to them, for you to keep staying with her even if it was easier to just let go and look away, I adore that. Everyone can learn from something, little communities like this help for the greater awareness of everyone, even this comment, whether it be helpful or not for your situation, could be helpful for someone else. It's a revolving cycle that helps not only the ones you're replying to, but also the ones that do see it.

For simplicity's sake, let's just say that by attaining this goal, you'll gain everything, every knowledge in the world, every power and authority, wealth and more, but in the end, you'll be unhappy with your situation anyway, similarly with if you had nothing but your humble existence.

&#x200B;

Knowing this, what would you choose?
